</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/24efcd80-e281-11f1-9484-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f74a33c4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/24efcd80-e281-11f1-9484-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f74a33c4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">284efdd8-e20a-11f1-ac80-bfc2bea6027a</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:07:47 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Sharon GAFFNEY</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Europe hunts for Palantir alternatives as militaries seek to limit US dependence</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe-hunts-for-palantir-alternatives-as-militaries-seek-to-limit-us-dependence</link><description>
As Europe's ​defence ministries look to limit dependency on American technology and major software company Palantir Technologies for critical military functions, a growing number of firms within the region are competing for their business. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Arthur Holland Michel, a journalist and researcher specialising in emerging military technologies.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/24efcd80-e281-11f1-9484-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f74a33c4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/24efcd80-e281-11f1-9484-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f74a33c4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3e7ed458-e209-11f1-b94c-5b50525dc19d</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:05:37 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>Hard bargain: Should Ukraine trade Donbas for NATO membership?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261116-should-ukraine-trade-donbas-for-nato-membership</link><description>
Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky is asserting that the United States has asked him about a straight-up swap: Russia gets the Donbas and the rest of Ukraine gets NATO membership – a guarantee against Moscow later having a second go and annexing the entire country. Whether or not the offer is real, serious or either side would accept it is another matter. We ask if the talk of partition signals some kind of turning point in a nearly five-year war that's at a brutal stalemate.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5dbb5ee6-e1ef-11f1-8f0e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/Anglais-2026-11-16T18-54-18-039Z.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5dbb5ee6-e1ef-11f1-8f0e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/Anglais-2026-11-16T18-54-18-039Z.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6c28c0b6-e1ec-11f1-8e7d-1184fd78518f</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:27:06 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>EU nations unveil Rwanda deal for migrant return hub</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/eu-nations-unveil-rwanda-deal-for-migrant-return-hub</link><description>
Five EU countries including Germany and Denmark on Monday unveiled a deal to establish a migrant deportation centre in Rwanda -- the first such facility outside the bloc. The group, also comprising Austria, Greece and the Netherlands, has been gunning to open a "return-hub" despite rights groups' concerns, after the European Union cleared the way with a recent toughening of migration rules. FRANCE 24's Dave Keating reports from Brussels.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7d2aecd0-e1f7-11f1-9c88-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261116-203438-203717-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7d2aecd0-e1f7-11f1-9c88-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261116-203438-203717-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8296031c-e1f7-11f1-b359-5f5eefe132e0</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:01:34 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Czech mass protests: Media is the first casualty 'when you want to attack democracy'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/czech-mass-protests-media-is-the-first-casualty-when-you-want-to-attack-democracy</link><description>
As hundreds of thousands of people gather in Prague to protest the Czech government's proposal to replace public broadcasting licence fees with direct state funding, Delano D'Souza is pleased to welcome Bára Stárek, Executive Director of Díky, že můžem. Stárek warns that the proposed changes offer "no real safeguards" against political influence, pointing to developments in Slovakia and Hungary. She also drew a parallel with the Velvet Revolution of 1989, recalling that "we didn't have a free media and we only had a state media" under communist rule. "Media is the first (line of) attack when you want to attack democracy," she said. With the legislation expected to reach Parliament in January, Stárek believes the demonstrations will continue if the government refuses to engage in any discussions.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/16a10508-e1e3-11f1-a5b1-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261116-161050-161723-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/16a10508-e1e3-11f1-a5b1-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261116-161050-161723-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1bb034c4-e1e3-11f1-aabe-d7578b65821b</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:33:00 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title><![CDATA[Russia's war on Ukraine: 'Americans want to have a deal whatever the cost for both Ukraine & Europe']]></title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/russia-s-war-on-ukraine-americans-want-to-have-a-deal-whatever-the-cost-for-both-ukraine-europe</link><description>
Delano D'Souza is pleased to welcome Grégoire Roos, Director of the Europe and Russia and Eurasia Programmes at Chatham House. As European countries prepare to take part in the next round of Ukraine peace talks, Roos questions whether having five European representatives could weaken their negotiating position. "The other option is to realize that it dilutes the negotiating power of Europe. And I would lean towards that," he says, proposing Finnish President Alexander Stubb as a possible representative. Roos also points to differences between Washington and European governments over negotiations with Russia. While "the Americans do absolutely want to have a deal whatever the cost for both Ukraine and Europe," he argues that European governments have little choice but to maintain pressure on Moscow, which has yet to abandon its "maximalist demands." 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/07f9db52-e1d8-11f1-97d4-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261116-150823-151954-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/07f9db52-e1d8-11f1-97d4-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261116-150823-151954-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0d5c55b6-e1d8-11f1-b217-97ae07c84fe8</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:22:49 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>New talks held between Kyiv, Europe and the US, Zelensky says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261116-live-600-ukrainian-drones-target-moscow-strike-crimea-bridge-halts-traffic</link><description>
European negotiators joined Ukrainian and US envoys online for the first time to strategise ending the Ukraine war, Ukrainian President Zelensky said Monday. This came after US President Donald Trump announced a plan to discuss the conflict during trilateral nuclear talks with Russia and China on the sidelines of the APEC summit in Shenzhen, China on Thursday. Read our liveblog to see how the day's events unfolded.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ee76ba2-de3f-11f1-879b-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26315756326943.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ee76ba2-de3f-11f1-879b-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26315756326943.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">68fa8a66-e186-11f1-b1b1-f7bba89d89da</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 06:52:27 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ee76ba2-de3f-11f1-879b-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26315756326943.jpg">© Virginia Mayo, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Hundreds of thousands of Czechs protest in Prague over public media funding plan</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261116-czechs-protest-prague-public-media-funding-plan</link><description>
Hundreds of thousands of Czechs rallied in Prague on Sunday against government plans to replace public media fees with direct state funding, which critics say threatens broadcasters’ independence. Organisers said turnout exceeded a March protest of more than 200,000, potentially making it the largest demonstration since the 1989 Velvet Revolution.
